1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,106
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:01,123 --> 00:00:02,148
Thanks for joining.

3
00:00:02,443 --> 00:00:03,047
I'm Artem.

4
00:00:03,865 --> 00:00:08,977
I've been a software engineer for
about 10 years, and I'm currently a

5
00:00:08,993 --> 00:00:13,263
senior software engineer at Amazon
working on AI shopping features.

6
00:00:14,729 --> 00:00:17,897
So, here's the topic
that I wanna talk about.

7
00:00:18,651 --> 00:00:24,469
Getting a model to produce good output in
a demo isn't hard anymore, and the work is

8
00:00:24,474 --> 00:00:31,073
now serving that output on a high-traffic
page without sl-slowing down the page and

9
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:33,305
without showing customers something wrong.

10
00:00:35,228 --> 00:00:36,841
I have one note on the scope.

11
00:00:36,841 --> 00:00:42,882
So this talk is about AI features that
live on a web page, the kind of a shopper

12
00:00:42,882 --> 00:00:45,165
loads in a browser, like a product page.

13
00:00:45,586 --> 00:00:49,779
If you're building a standalone chatbot
or a background agent, some of this

14
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:54,694
still applies, but a traditional web
page is what I have in mind for this talk

15
00:00:55,694 --> 00:00:59,268
So you probably know the
standard production toolkit.

16
00:00:59,375 --> 00:01:04,950
It's timeouts, caching, canaries,
feature flags, dashboards, A/B tests.

17
00:01:05,295 --> 00:01:09,688
So LLM features add three
problems on top of it.

18
00:01:10,385 --> 00:01:11,862
The first is latency.

19
00:01:12,564 --> 00:01:16,555
Google's guidance is that a page's
main content should appear within two

20
00:01:16,555 --> 00:01:21,305
point five seconds, and that the first
byte from the server should arrive

21
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:24,014
in under zero point eight seconds.

22
00:01:24,882 --> 00:01:29,145
A fast model starts streaming in a
few hundred milliseconds, but a full

23
00:01:29,236 --> 00:01:35,247
response usually takes several seconds,
and the slow tail runs well past that.

24
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:38,442
The second is quality.

25
00:01:38,882 --> 00:01:43,547
Bad output usually comes back
well-formed with a successful status,

26
00:01:43,557 --> 00:01:48,021
so your error rate stays flat while
customers read something wrong.

27
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:50,618
The third is cost.

28
00:01:51,093 --> 00:01:55,224
Every call costs money and at
page view volume, that can add up.

29
00:01:56,789 --> 00:02:06,234
So the four lessons that follow are
about these three problems So I've

30
00:02:06,234 --> 00:02:09,334
grouped this into these four lessons.

31
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,245
They follow the life of a feature.

32
00:02:11,766 --> 00:02:16,286
So it's deciding when to generate,
serving the output, checking it,

33
00:02:16,343 --> 00:02:18,348
and connecting to the assistant.

34
00:02:19,545 --> 00:02:22,163
All of this is model agnostic

35
00:02:23,163 --> 00:02:26,179
To keep this simple, I'll
use one example throughout.

36
00:02:26,998 --> 00:02:29,464
Ridgeline Coffee, a made-up store.

37
00:02:30,486 --> 00:02:35,172
Ridgeline wants three LLM
features on its product pages.

38
00:02:35,575 --> 00:02:37,416
The first is a quick take.

39
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:42,494
It's three short highlights
about the coffee written by an

40
00:02:42,514 --> 00:02:47,883
LLM It uses product details and
reviews to get this information.

41
00:02:48,115 --> 00:02:50,148
It shows on every product page.

42
00:02:51,041 --> 00:02:53,283
The second is suggested questions.

43
00:02:54,054 --> 00:02:57,821
A few questions picked for this
shop are like, "Is it similar to

44
00:02:57,821 --> 00:02:59,694
the Colombia I bought last time?"

45
00:03:00,677 --> 00:03:06,810
They also show on every product page, and
tapping one asks the store's AI assistant.

46
00:03:07,755 --> 00:03:09,900
The third is an Ask button.

47
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:14,371
It opens the same assistant, which
already knows which coffee you're looking

48
00:03:14,372 --> 00:03:19,708
at, so you can ask your own questions
like, "Will this work for espresso?"

49
00:03:20,708 --> 00:03:26,257
So these are the two kinds of AI
features I'll keep coming back to.

50
00:03:27,004 --> 00:03:28,934
The quick take is pre-computed.

51
00:03:29,131 --> 00:03:33,986
It's generated ahead of time, and
the page only reads stored text.

52
00:03:34,889 --> 00:03:39,816
The suggested questions
and ask button are live.

53
00:03:40,694 --> 00:03:43,875
They call the model while
the shopper is on the page.

54
00:03:44,758 --> 00:03:48,453
Each kind runs into different
problems, so for every lesson,

55
00:03:48,468 --> 00:03:50,712
I'll say which kind it is about.

56
00:03:51,712 --> 00:03:54,277
Lesson one: precompute by default.

57
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:59,798
So the problem is when you're doing
a demo, right, you're most likely

58
00:03:59,799 --> 00:04:02,445
just doing a live call to LLM.

59
00:04:03,457 --> 00:04:09,534
But then if you just put it to production,
now every page would wait for a model

60
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:16,696
call that takes seconds and costs
money, and for some features, the output

61
00:04:16,731 --> 00:04:18,407
would be the same for every shopper.

62
00:04:19,329 --> 00:04:23,601
And so before building a feature,
it's worth asking whether it has to

63
00:04:23,608 --> 00:04:25,832
be generated while the shopper waits.

64
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:28,890
So there's three questions that decide it.

65
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:32,688
Does the output depend on the
shopper or what they typed?

66
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:35,162
How often do inputs change?

67
00:04:35,291 --> 00:04:37,816
And is anyone waiting for it?

68
00:04:38,741 --> 00:04:42,777
So the quick take is the
same for every shopper.

69
00:04:43,152 --> 00:04:47,544
It changes only when the
product or its reviews change,

70
00:04:47,995 --> 00:04:49,244
and nobody's waiting for it.

71
00:04:49,830 --> 00:04:55,104
So we generate it in a background job,
run the checks, and store the default.

72
00:04:56,796 --> 00:05:00,827
At page load, the page does a key lookup.

73
00:05:01,042 --> 00:05:05,201
The ask button can't be
precomputed because the response

74
00:05:05,205 --> 00:05:06,625
depends on the question.

75
00:05:07,291 --> 00:05:11,844
It runs live, but only for the
shoppers who click, which is

76
00:05:11,844 --> 00:05:14,604
usually a small share of page views.

77
00:05:16,121 --> 00:05:20,606
Suggested questions depend on the
shopper, and there are too many

78
00:05:20,609 --> 00:05:24,475
shoppers and product pairs to generate
them all ahead of time, so they

79
00:05:24,476 --> 00:05:27,334
run live too on every page view.

80
00:05:28,339 --> 00:05:31,135
So keep that in mind for lesson four.

81
00:05:32,241 --> 00:05:37,188
So this works because a
precomputed feature puts no

82
00:05:37,188 --> 00:05:39,299
model call on the page at all.

83
00:05:40,046 --> 00:05:45,076
Latency becomes just a key
lookup, and cost follows product

84
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:46,813
changes instead of traffic.

85
00:05:47,935 --> 00:05:53,320
So the LLM part becomes a
content pipeline, which is a,

86
00:05:53,917 --> 00:05:55,581
an easier to deal with problem.

87
00:05:56,969 --> 00:06:00,801
One thing to get right
is when to regenerate.

88
00:06:01,537 --> 00:06:06,794
If you regenerate on every new review,
a popular product burns model calls

89
00:06:06,854 --> 00:06:09,315
all day for output that barely changes.

90
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:16,246
So a threshold, like a minimum number of
new reviews or a change to the product

91
00:06:16,249 --> 00:06:19,211
details, keeps costs tied to real change.

92
00:06:19,965 --> 00:06:28,635
I start from precomputed and see if the
feature really needs to be real time

93
00:06:29,635 --> 00:06:33,310
Lesson two, keep the model
off the critical path.

94
00:06:33,599 --> 00:06:39,685
So the problem is that a model call
takes seconds and sometimes fails.

95
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:47,277
If the page waits for it, the page is as
slow and as fragile as this model call.

96
00:06:47,853 --> 00:06:51,127
So the usual protection, a
timeout, doesn't help much here.

97
00:06:51,553 --> 00:06:56,373
A timeout short enough to protect the
page would cut off most of model calls.

98
00:06:57,284 --> 00:07:00,918
So the fix depends on the kind of feature.

99
00:07:01,789 --> 00:07:06,755
A pre-computed feature already reads
stored text, so the open question is

100
00:07:06,777 --> 00:07:09,204
what to show when nothing good is stored.

101
00:07:10,346 --> 00:07:12,642
Decide that before you
write a single prompt.

102
00:07:12,710 --> 00:07:15,897
I think of it as a ladder.

103
00:07:16,435 --> 00:07:18,090
So fresh content first.

104
00:07:18,214 --> 00:07:22,356
If that's missing or failed a check,
the last good version you saved.

105
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:27,952
If there's none, a plain non-AI default,
like the product's existing bullet points.

106
00:07:28,385 --> 00:07:30,702
And as a last resort,
just hide the feature.

107
00:07:31,623 --> 00:07:36,507
For live features, render the page
first and call the model afterwards.

108
00:07:36,949 --> 00:07:40,613
The Ask button calls it
only after the shopper asks.

109
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:46,113
Suggested questions fill in after
the page has loaded, and if they're

110
00:07:46,265 --> 00:07:49,672
slow or fail, that space stays empty.

111
00:07:50,656 --> 00:07:52,632
For Ask, stream the response.

112
00:07:53,063 --> 00:07:57,383
The first words appear long before
the full response would, and streaming

113
00:07:57,385 --> 00:08:02,329
keeps long responses from hitting
fixed timeouts in proxies and gateways.

114
00:08:03,577 --> 00:08:06,387
This works because the page
never waits on the model.

115
00:08:06,869 --> 00:08:10,879
Whether the model is slow, failing,
or down, the page loads, and

116
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:13,086
the worst case is a plainer page

117
00:08:14,087 --> 00:08:18,946
Lesson three, check outputs
before customers see them So the

118
00:08:18,947 --> 00:08:23,607
problem is that wrong output from
a model could look like success.

119
00:08:24,154 --> 00:08:29,589
It's well-formed, the call returns fine,
and your error dashboard stays flat,

120
00:08:30,504 --> 00:08:32,588
and the company owns it either way.

121
00:08:32,676 --> 00:08:38,058
So there was an interesting case in two
thousand twenty-four, a Cana- Canadian

122
00:08:38,065 --> 00:08:44,067
tribunal ruled against Air Canada
after its chatbot told a customer that

123
00:08:44,067 --> 00:08:49,413
he could claim a bereavement discount
after his trip, which contradicted

124
00:08:49,413 --> 00:08:51,391
the airline's own policy page.

125
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:56,748
But the tribunal held the airline
responsible for what the chatbot said, the

126
00:08:56,748 --> 00:08:59,891
same as for any other page on its site.

127
00:09:01,552 --> 00:09:06,426
So the fix for a precomputed
feature like quick take is check

128
00:09:06,429 --> 00:09:08,335
four things before storing it.

129
00:09:09,432 --> 00:09:14,634
Format so it renders, grounding
so every claim traces back to

130
00:09:14,635 --> 00:09:20,032
the product data or a review,
consistency with the rest of the page.

131
00:09:20,301 --> 00:09:26,263
A highlight that says the coffee ships
ground on a page that says whole bean

132
00:09:26,288 --> 00:09:32,521
only is the kind of the problem that
Air Canada had at a smaller scale.

133
00:09:33,586 --> 00:09:34,902
And the content policy.

134
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:38,958
Format and policy are mostly simple rules.

135
00:09:39,424 --> 00:09:44,895
Grounding and consistency often need a
second model call to judge the first.

136
00:09:45,590 --> 00:09:52,317
Anything that fails goes down the
fallback ladder from lesson two This

137
00:09:52,319 --> 00:09:56,417
works because that output is caught
before it's stored, so it never reaches

138
00:09:56,420 --> 00:10:01,905
the page, and the extra model call is
affordable because you pay for it once per

139
00:10:01,905 --> 00:10:05,310
product change, not once per page view.

140
00:10:07,135 --> 00:10:11,659
Live responses are harder because
there's no time to run all of that

141
00:10:11,696 --> 00:10:16,337
before the first word appears,
so the fix comes in layers.

142
00:10:16,988 --> 00:10:21,798
Keep the model working from the product's
own data so grounding is built in.

143
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:28,677
Run cheap rule-based checks on
the stream and stop or respl--

144
00:10:28,744 --> 00:10:31,085
replace response that fails one.

145
00:10:31,994 --> 00:10:37,243
Afterwards, judge a sample of real
responses with the same checks

146
00:10:37,510 --> 00:10:39,499
you use for precomputed content.

147
00:10:40,494 --> 00:10:45,519
That catches problems in aggregate,
not one response at a time, which

148
00:10:45,521 --> 00:10:49,849
is why live features also need a
narrower scope and an easy way for

149
00:10:49,850 --> 00:10:52,137
shoppers to report a bad response.

150
00:10:53,551 --> 00:10:58,234
For both precomputed and live
features, keep an eval set, a fixed

151
00:10:58,237 --> 00:11:02,266
set of real products with known
good and known bad outputs rerun

152
00:11:02,270 --> 00:11:04,283
on every prompt or model change.

153
00:11:04,898 --> 00:11:08,651
And in production, watch the rejection
rate, especially while prompt

154
00:11:08,670 --> 00:11:10,946
or model change is rolling out.

155
00:11:11,706 --> 00:11:15,838
The page will look healthy because
the fallbacks cover for failures, so

156
00:11:16,228 --> 00:11:20,395
moving rejection rate is often your
first sign that something changed

157
00:11:21,395 --> 00:11:25,602
Lesson four: put one service
in front of the assistant.

158
00:11:26,215 --> 00:11:29,322
This one is about live features.

159
00:11:29,845 --> 00:11:34,643
So for Ridgeline Coffee, two features
call the same assistant, the Ask

160
00:11:34,698 --> 00:11:37,062
button and the suggested questions.

161
00:11:37,877 --> 00:11:40,203
The first problem is duplicated rules.

162
00:11:40,532 --> 00:11:45,855
When each feature calls the assistant
directly, each one keeps its own copy

163
00:11:45,857 --> 00:11:50,582
of the rules, which pages and shoppers
are eligible, which languages are

164
00:11:50,585 --> 00:11:55,250
supported, which timeout to use, and
what to show when the call fails.

165
00:11:56,162 --> 00:12:00,314
Those copies could drift apart
into features on the same page

166
00:12:00,314 --> 00:12:01,819
start behaving differently.

167
00:12:02,825 --> 00:12:04,809
Second problem is capacity.

168
00:12:06,416 --> 00:12:10,798
Let's say the assistant was first
built for the Ask button, so

169
00:12:10,798 --> 00:12:14,998
it's sized for the shoppers who
click it, which is a small share.

170
00:12:15,728 --> 00:12:19,473
Suggested questions appear on
every product page, so they call

171
00:12:19,482 --> 00:12:21,597
the assistant on every page view.

172
00:12:22,384 --> 00:12:25,546
Launching them can multiply
the assistant's traffic.

173
00:12:26,704 --> 00:12:32,275
So the fix is a small service between
the features and the assistant.

174
00:12:32,928 --> 00:12:36,878
Features don't call the assistant
directly, they call the service.

175
00:12:37,417 --> 00:12:42,183
And the service, it holds the eligibility
rules, sets the timeout, and returns

176
00:12:42,184 --> 00:12:44,267
a fallback when the assistant fails.

177
00:12:45,256 --> 00:12:49,652
It also gives each feature a
traffic budget and rate limits

178
00:12:49,872 --> 00:12:51,964
any feature that goes over it.

179
00:12:53,462 --> 00:12:57,378
I make it a service rather than a
shared library because a library can't

180
00:12:57,399 --> 00:13:00,547
enforce one budget across every caller.

181
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:07,180
This works because the rules live
in one place, so they cannot drift.

182
00:13:07,907 --> 00:13:13,049
Every calls goes through one door, so no
feature can send page scale traffic to the

183
00:13:13,049 --> 00:13:15,438
assistant without someone planning for it.

184
00:13:16,801 --> 00:13:21,030
If the assistant slows down, the service
returns fallbacks, and the pages are fine.

185
00:13:21,345 --> 00:13:25,991
If a feature misbehaves, its budget
stops it before it reaches the assistant

186
00:13:26,991 --> 00:13:32,021
So if there's one thing you remember
from this talk, it's calling the

187
00:13:32,022 --> 00:13:36,611
model is easy, and most of the
work is just a system around it.

188
00:13:37,270 --> 00:13:38,605
So that's all from me.

189
00:13:38,617 --> 00:13:39,627
Thanks for watching.

190
00:13:39,972 --> 00:13:44,021
If you're shipping LLM features too, I'd
like to hear how it's going, so find me on

191
00:13:44,030 --> 00:13:46,993
LinkedIn or on the Conf42 Discord.

192
00:13:47,885 --> 00:13:49,383
Enjoy the rest of the conference.
